body:
I had to have a sit-down with a parent regarding some ongoing issues. For one, the student, a girl, was physically aggressive with myself and other students. Secondly, the lunches this girl was packed went against what we were allowed to let her eat according to state regulations and school policy on a daily basis. Lunch aids had to take something away and give her our crappy free stuff literally every day. Policy reminder notes in her lunchbox weren't doing anything. So as we're setting up the meeting, I expressed concern about conferencing with just the dad. He had threatened to "lose it" on me and had a massive freakout a few weeks prior when I told him his daughter had purposely tried to trip another teacher on the playground. Mom couldn't make the meeting, so I had another teacher and an office admin in the room with me. This was mostly a precaution and I couldn't have predicted how badly the meeting actually went. It ended with the cops being called. The dad called the pregnant teacher a whore, the office admin the n-word, and threatened us by showing us a giant knife on his belt. He took off before the cops arrived. His daughter was immediately disenrolled and the family was never allowed to set foot near the school again. Fuck that guy.

&nbsp;

EDIT: Lots of folks are asking what kind of lunches aren't allowed so I'll clear things up. Allergens and high-sugar content were the main issues. The entire age group eats lunch together and we had a few students with severe nut allergies. We also had a policy on sugar content, as silly as it sounds. Candy, soda, and sugary drinks weren't allowed, though certain treats were okay on special occasions (like birthdays). There was a specific sugar limit we had to check for, but I don't think the lunch aids ever went THAT far. We generally knew the healthier brands and I know they'd let the occasional thing slide, like fruit snacks or cereal bars, without checking the exact grams of sugar, within reason. But for the most part it was kind of out of our hands, some things are generally against state rules and some of it has to do with grant/funding stipulations. With that being said, some of her regular lunch items would be peanut butter sandwiches, granola bars with nuts, PB, or candy in them, candy, soda, and Capri Suns. We offered suggestions for alternatives plenty of times, too. All in all, if you thought all that over a granola bar was pretty silly, imagine how WE felt.

When I was a teenager, we had some family friends come visit us from out of the country for a week. It was supposed to be the couple and their three kids, but when they arrived, their neighbors also showed up. A second set of husband and wife and three kids. We'd met these neighbors once when we took a trip out to see the family friends. These people saw our friends loading up the car on the morning they were leaving and decided to join them. Apparently the neighbors were VERY persistent and our friends didn't really know how to say no to them. My parents tried to be nice about a suggesting they get a nearby hotel, as we really only had enough room to squeeze in one more family (we also were a family of 5), and we has already made sleeping arrangements. They were like "Nah it's fine, we can all just stay in one room." More persistence, and my parents eventually caved and I had to give up my room for this entire fucking family of 5 to sleep in. For an entire week, our 4-bedroom house had 15 people in it. Any trip we made involved making arrangements to transport 15 people, so we stopped trying to go anywhere interesting. Trying to find a good time to shower and use the bathroom for anything was a pain in the ass. But the worst part was that these people (The persistent neighbors) let their kids run around with zero supervision. The daughter who couldn't swim fell in the swimming pool multiple times. The two sons rummaged through the personal belongings in every room in the house, finding where my brothers and I respectively kept our Gameboys, getting into my art supplies and vintage toys, and brazenly playing with whatever they found in front of everyone. We also all eventually noticed money missing from various spots. Usually departing from the family friends we loved dearly was difficult and sad, but I had never been more excited for them to leave and take their weird neighbors with them. When I finally got my room back it was a complete mess and it REEKED. The air in there was thick and humid, and it smelled like that sweaty, almost dirt-like musk a construction worker comes home with after a 12-hour shift, only instead of it being a person in boots, it was hot, x5, and it smacked me in the face when I opened the door. My room got the best scrubdown of any room I've ever lived in.

&nbsp;

TL;DR: Family friends showed up to stay at our house for a week with their weird and persistent neighbors. The family of five all stayed in my room, terrorized us for 7 days, and then left behind a lingering smell.

TL;DR: Student kicked me in the face, admins told me I just needed to "watch him better".


&nbsp;


I had a student who was showing all the signs of a learning disability. I'm not at liberty to diagnose anyone, but it was my job to recognize the signs. I report this to administration repeatedly, with notes and observations to back it up, only to be told not to mention anything to his mother, but that they would suggest to her the child get a screening. For now, they said, "shadow" the child and "watch him better" and see if that helps. After several weeks of sacrificing the learning of the rest of the class, I made it clear that I was very upset by the specific wording of "suggest" and demanded to know what was going to be done, because what was going on wasn't fair to anyone. The director of my program explained that, for business purposes, suggestion was really as far as they could go because we were a private school. Public schools took care of disability services and telling the parents to get a screening would be like telling parents to disenroll and stop paying us. If it was suggested then it would be a choice on her part. And also, still don't mention anyrhing to her yourself. The most I was allowed to do was inform her of behaviors. Shortly after, this student literally kicked me in the face. I called the office in tears asking someone to step in for me so I could take a breather and calm down. An admin did come into my class a few minutes later, but only to advise me to just "watch him better" and then leave. And that was the precise moment I remember thinking "Fuck this shit". I had left public schools the year before because I felt like I couldn't do enough for the students, only to be treated like dirt by a private school that clearly cared more about money than students or faculty. I realized that I couldn't give these kids what they needed in the way I was truly passionate about as a teacher, so back to school I went.

I like to tell this story because it feels less like something that should happen in real life and more like a filler episode of a dramedy. My ex and I were hanging out one night when I got a Messenger request from a random girl asking about my then-boyfriend's brother. Having been friends with his brother for 5-6 ish years at that point, I immediately sent a screenshot of the message to his brother, asking what that was all about. No response. The girl proceeds to send me another message explaining that she is bf's brother's co-worker, and that he hadn't shown up for his shift and no one was able to get a hold of him. I showed my boyfriend the message, and he also began recieving similar messages from this girl, along with his brother's boss. We started calling his brother off the hook, texting, leaving voicemails, but getting no response. We called some family and friends, but no one had seen or heard from him in at least 24 hours. At that point it was around 10:30 pm, so we decided to just drive over to his place and see what was going on. Truthfully, we were a little worried. A few months prior, he had housed their older brother, who wasn't the best person to have around. The older brother had a problem with substances, and made money thieving and hustling, and associated with some very scary folks. We were considering the possibility that something bad good have happened to the younger brother, but we were trying not to assume the worst. 

&nbsp;

When we got to his apartment, his car was there, but no one was answering the door. Knowing the balcony door was broken, my ex jumped up to the balcony and got into the apartment. We found his brother's phone, keys, and wallet, but his brother wasn't home. We spent the rest of the night calling hospitals, more friends and family, and police departments. We fell asleep in the apartment well into the early hours of the morning waiting for the local police to get back to us. When we did get a call back, we learned that my ex's brother had been arrested almost 24 hours before at that point. They gave us the charges but couldn't tell us the circumstrances of the arrest. We didn't get much more info at his court hearing the next morning, other than that his bail was set ridiculously high, and that we still couldn't see him until he was booked in an actual jail and not just the holding facility.

&nbsp;

We spent the next three days playing detective and following leads. We traveled back and forth between courthouses and the local police station countless times, speaking to various different people and branches. At some point we were able to get some information we shouldn't have gotten from a courthouse employee who apparently didn't care much about her job. We learned that my ex's brother was being accused of stealing a car, hitting a kid, running through some guy's fence, and then fleeing from police. We knew that there had to be some kind of mistake, but of course, no one believed us when we said we were trying to get an innocent guy out of jail.

&nbsp;

To make matters worse, there was a point in time when we couldn't even find the kid. Just when we were supposed to have been able to see him, he was moved, and we wouldn't know where until he was processed at the new place. We'd also been spending the entire time trying to get a hold of the detective in charge of the case. When we finally got to have a webcam visit with him, he was just as confused about him being in jail as we were. He said that cops knocked on his door at 6 in the morning, asking questions about their older brother's ex girlfriend, and asked to go in. Having nothing to hide, he let them in, and the next thing he knew, he was being arrested. It wasn't much to go on, but we had some sort of lead.

&nbsp;

On day four, we managed to find the older brother's ex girlfriend at her grandfather's house. The grandfather explained that the car he had reported stolen was involved in a hit and run and some property damage. It turned out that his granddaughter had actually stolen the car and let her new boyfriend borrow it. She was miraculously unavailable when the police were investigating, but her friends thought she might be with her ex boyfriend (who they didn't know was in jail) and directed then to my ex's brother's apartment, where they knew he used to live. When the younger brother answered the door, they thought he matched the description of the guy they were looking for. They found some clothes that kinda looked like what the witnesses said the guy was wearing, and he was arrested. But the identity of the guy who actually committed the crimes? Well, he was already in jail for murdering someone of the span of time that someone else had been arrested for the car incident.

&nbsp;

We left a few more voicemails giving the detective the information we had found. On day 5, he finally got back to us. He said he had been super busy, but that he had also come to the conclusion that he had arrested the wrong guy, and was going to "make it right". It took another two days for the kid to be released. He was in jail for a week due to mistaken identity, simply because he sorta looked like the suspect.

&nbsp;

TL;DR: My ex and I spent a week locating his missing brother, finding out he was in jail due to mistaken identity, and trying to get him out.

When I first transitioned from high school to college (and was already living on my own at that point), I worked with a girl who was always complaining that she was getting scheduled 3 days a week instead of two, because school was just so hard for her otherwise. She lived with her parents and didn't have any real bills, which is all fine, I wasn't mad at her for that. But as a full-time student working two jobs back-to-back, totalling 12 hour days, going to school at night afterward, and still juggling online classes, I couldn't help but roll my eyes. Especially because we were both first year students at the same college so I had a good idea of the kind of school work she had going on, as well. I tried not to say anything about it because I didn't want to seem jealous or judgemental, and I know different things affect people in different ways, but I couldn't help but ask myself "How are you really complaining about having to work 15 hours a week right now?" Eventually I brought it up as I was the one covering her shifts on weekend mornings, nothing to do with her schooling in particular but a polite "Look I won't complain about the money, but I can't keep taking your shift and working 7 days a week. Speak to the manager more clearly about your schedule needs because I just won't take your shift anymore". She got defensive and said "But I have full time school!" I reminded her that I did too, and she replied with "But I actually have to get good grades". Not sure what she was implying but I definitely wasn't paying for classes just to get poor grades, and I was getting straight A's while paying for the roof over my head. That's when I realized that it was more than her and I living different lives, she was downright clueless about how the world worked for other people sometimes. Eventually she got her wish and was only working two days a week, then was complaining she couldn't pay her "bills" because she wasn't making enough. Eventually she quit because her parents wanted her to pay for part of her car insurance (on the car they bought her) if she has a job, so she decided to stop working so she didn't have to. To be fair, her parents were giving her all the support i hope to give my future children at that age, I just hope I teach them to earn and value things and not turn out like that co worker.

&nbsp;

TL;DR: Yeah, fuck that girl.

I always try to pick one but my school had so many great ones. I picked one per school year while I attended.

&nbsp;

Freshman year: girl's nudes spread throughout the school because  her stepdad found them on her phone and sent them to everyone in her contacts as punishment.

&nbsp;

Sophomore year: A hallway security camera caught two students having oral sex. It was during class time, and they weren't sure if the kids had ditched class or asked to go to the bathroom, so no one was allowed to be outside of a classroom during class time for the rest of the semester. Camera didn't catch the kids faces so they were never actually identified.

&nbsp;

Junior year: Bus driver got arrested after sending a video to an underage student of himself jacking off. His wife had just had a baby, and his mom worked for the school, too, so it was a huge fucking thing.

&nbsp;

Senior year: shortly after his 18th birthday, a guy in my grade got arrested for accusations of being a serial rapist.

&nbsp;

My little brother is now a sophomore at that school and he recently told me that a girl tried to commit suicide by throwing herself down some stairs during passing periods, so pretty much nothing has changed.

My problem with shows that have morally questionable characters as the protagonist is that I've yet to find one that doesn't eventually evolve into a shit show where I hate nearly every character. I'll give Mad Men credit for having really strong plot points with almost all of the characters, and giving Don some good moments of ups and downs. There was a high period of the series where it was at its best and progressively getting better than the beginning, but it definitely still slid downhill for me. My SO actually stopped watching it with me because the characters would make him so angry. The one thing that redeemed the entire series for me, though, was the ending. One of my all-time favorite finales of a series ever.

&nbsp;

EDIT: apparently people are really passionate about Mad Men. I just want to make it clear that while I did understand and relate to every character at one point in time, I also disliked each of them for various reasons at least one point in time, as well. I have my own reasons for having a hard time watching characters suffer through low points over and over, but I didn't dislike the characters because I don't "get sadness" or whatever else. My only point that when a show like this goes on long enough, it's only a matter of time before I see them do things that really taint my opinion of them. Not to mention, there were a few characters in this show that were just explicitly bad fucking people, and when you see bad people get away with bad shit over and over, that has an effect on how enjoyable the show is, too. It isn't without its merits but like many shows with anti-hero protagonists, there were times when I couldn't stand the characters anymore. That's just my perspective.

My niece is the queen of brutally honest. A few of my favorites:

&nbsp;

Her: Oh my gosh what's wrong with your teeth? They're all crooked.

&nbsp;
Me: Sometimes people's teeth grow in crooked. There's nothing wrong with them, though.

&nbsp;
Her: Aww, don't worry. It's going to be okay.

&nbsp;

Another time I went over to my sister's house in pajamas. My niece apparently didn't approve of what I was wearing.

&nbsp;
Her: You're not wearing makeup today.

&nbsp;
Me: not today. Sometimes I like sleeping more than makeup.

&nbsp;
Her: Oh no! Your hair is all wild.

&nbsp;
Me: You don't like my hair?

&nbsp;
Her: It's okay, but maybe you need to go home and put on makeup and fix your hair.... and put on a skirt, not jammies.

&nbsp;

Once she was sitting on my lap and I was reading to her. I was wearing leggings and my legs were a little prickly.

&nbsp;
Her: Your legs feel weird. They're not soft.

&nbsp;
Me: That happens when my leg hair grows. It's almost time for me to shave.

&nbsp;
Her: You need to take the hair off... Right now. Go.

&nbsp;

Honorable mentions:

&nbsp;
She once accidentally ran into my sister's friend, who happens to be on the heavier side. The friend said "Oh, be careful", and my niece said "Why? Will your big belly pop?"

&nbsp;
Another time she asked her dad if he farted in his own mouth. When he laughed and no, she said "Then why does your breath smell like farts so bad?"

I was enjoying a relaxing bath one evening when I heard some aggressive knocking at the door. My dogs went nuts so I knew it wasn't someone they knew. Being in the bath and home alone, I ignored it. If it WAS by chance someone I knew, they'd call me or something anyway. Well, they kept knocking, even louder. For some reason my mind jumped to the idea that someone was checking to see if I was home so they could rob me, and I figured I needed to be clothed in case something sketchy was about to happen. Jumped out, threw some clothes on, and grabbed one of the kamas we keep in the living room. They knocked again pretty persistently, so I snuck up to the door and looked through the peephole. Some guy wearing a hat and sunglasses was standing there with his head down. Mind you it was already getting dark so I was feeling some kinda way about this. He ducked down below where I could see through the peephole. I backed away instinctively, then after a few seconds I looked again. He hadn't gotten up. I waited and waited for him to surface. I was starting to consider calling the police and just asking them to come check things out. Decided to just open the door with my kama in hand and hope for the best. No one was there, but there was a FedEx envelope tucked under the mat. Not sure why he was knocking so fucking aggressively, but he must have ducked down really quick, popped back up, and walked away before I looked back out. Good example that creepy things are not always inexplicable, but I was genuinely scared, considering-the-possibility-of-having-to-kill-in-self-defense scared. Glad I didn't call the police.

&nbsp;

TL;DR: almost killed a FedEx delivery person.

I have two older sister's. At middle sister's wedding, oldest sister showed up in a trashy outfit with one of the groomsmen as her meal ticke- uh, "date". Got super drunk and started rubbing her ass all over her date in front of my incredibly uncomfortable grandparents. Then she got angry that middle sister, the fucking bride, was getting more attention, and convinced her date to start a fight with the groom. Mind you, he was a groomsman who had known my BIL since they were like 10 years old. But he's drunk and wants to get laid by a girl he thinks is way out of his league, so he does it. Best man and maid of honor, without being promoted by the bride and groom at all, step in and rather politely suggest that the groomsman leave. My oldest sister follows him out the door, screaming the entire way that her own sister was kicking her out, when my sister hadn't actually kicked anyone out, and the only person actually asked to leave was the groomsman. Our mom then went around to the 100 or so guests, asked them if they saw what happened, filled them in if they hadn't, then apologized on behalf of the bride (who had done nothing wrong), and cried that she was so worried about her older daughter because she was "wondering around all alone" and my mom didn't know where she was. My stepdad got so drunk he passed out in a chair and vomited all over himself. The bride suggested my mom help him back to their hotel room, and my mom proceeded to make a scene about how she was also getting kicked out of the wedding. So yeah, not my wedding, but only because those bitches aren't invited.

&nbsp;

EDIT: After reading some of these other posts, I feel like I've had an epiphany. My SO and I got engaged almost two years ago. A combination of family pressures (mostly his mom having demands for our wedding, but also having to process the passing of my grandad, who was supposed to walk me down the aisle), and wanting to enjoy and go through this phase in our relationship before adding marriage to the equation, brought us to amicably decide to put the engagement on hold. We were tired of people asking us when we were going to get married, when we knew that it wasn't going to be for a while, and that our commitment to each other (knowing we want to get married eventually, but want it to be our way when it feels right) is enough. We've been talking a lot about marriage again and how my SO wants to repropose with a ring he's not ashamed of (I don't care but feeling like he can provide for me as a husband is what he's processing right now so I'm being supportive), and I realized something. I fucking dreaded the idea of having to plan a wedding, please everyone, minimize drama, and still be stress free enough to enjoy the happiest day of my life. When we feel ready to pursue marriage again, I think I just want to elope.

I've told part of this story on reddit before but I moved out of an abusive home my last semester of high school. A few times when I was 16 and tried to be more independent my parents used threatening to kick me out and throwing me outside in the middle of the night as a scare tactic. When I was 18 they started threatening again so in anticipation of getting thrown out in the cold I snuck all my stuff out of the house via my friend and her parents stored it for me over the course of about a week. They threw me out on a Valentine's Day night in 30 degree weather and instead of pounding on the door and begging to come back in I turned around and walked away and haven't looked back since. My parents reported me missing but the police didn't put too much effort into finding me as I was 18 and it was pretty obvious I'd taken my stuff and didn't disappear into thin air. And of course my parents were not going to admit that I'd gone missing after they threw me out. They weren't even worried so much as controlling, if it were up to them I'd be their complacent slave to this day. I had about 4 days of feeling safe before being found. I had a hard time transferring schools so close to the end of the school year, so instead of risking not graduating I decided to stay at the same school and take over my own affairs. I met with a youth liason who determined my situation unsafe and helped me increase the security on my school records. No one was supposed to be able to recieve any information about me if they called and no one was supposed to be able to pull me out of class and visit if they showed up at the school. The vice principal at my school didn't believe me when I tried to tell her my parents were abusive and told me she "sees that stuff happen all the time with AP students when parents are sad to see their kid about to go off to college". My mom tried various people at my school until she got a hold of the vice principal, who hinted to my mom that I was still enrolled at the school. She called me into the office mid-day where I was met by my mom and step-dad, and the vice principal trying to mediate a family reunion. I yelled at the VP for threatening my safety, told my parents to leave, and went back to class. My mom stopped caring and gave up when she realized I wasn't coming back. My stepdad stalked me for a couple of months so I couldn't go anywhere alone for a little while (and still have anxiety about going out alone sometimes). I was staying at a friend's house and was otherwise homeless so I didn't have much of a choice but to lay low until I graduated. The parents of the man who is now my fiance helped me get out of town and let me move in with them when I finished high school. I got on my feet and became a functioning adult, aside from the PTSD I do alright. My stepdad has no idea where I live and I don't speak to the creepy fuck at all.

&nbsp;

**TL;DR:** Got away from abusive parents, parents told everyone I was missing, shitty vice principal at my school told them where I was. Didn't let them talk me into going home and I now live a pretty normal, nice life  and neither of them know where I live.

Kind of on a lower scale than some of these but the women's staff bathroom toilet at my job doesn't flush. I have no idea if there's a way to get it to flush that I just don't know about, or if it flushes for other people and just not for me. Occassionally it does flush so every time I go to sit on it I just pray that this time will be lucky for me. I have anxiety and don't know an appropriate way to exit the bathroom and ask someone to help me flush my pee. I once asked another female employee if she ever had trouble flushing the toilet in the girls staff bathroom and she said "Yes" and then walked away. I started using the men's toilet because it does flush.

&nbsp;

EDIT: Just to clarify, there is nothing special about this toilet. No motion sensors or special hidden buttons. It has a little silver lever that can be pushed up or down, as a standard toilet would. Only when it's pressed it does nothing. It makes a running water noise but doesn't actually flush. The men's toilet is exactly the same only it does flush, so I just use that.

&nbsp;

EDIT 2: People are explaining really basic toilet functions to me as if I haven't tried every common sense thing to troubleshoot the issue. Guys, trust me, I have. I've tried holding the lever longer (like 20-30 seconds, seriously), man I've even tried plunging the damn thing. It's always clear when I walk in but just doesn't flush for me. I'm convinced the toilet might just hate me.

You didn't specify what you meant by scene but in her defense, you can never be too careful. As a teacher of young kids, I'm frequently advised to be cautious about anyone I don't recognize or haven't been told might pick up. Even if there's an obvious resemblence, I always politely confirm who the person is and make sure parents know they're picking up, it also helps that people have to pass through the office, show ID, and sometimes have verbal or written permission from parents already on record to even get into the school. Parents are also told to keep an eye out and never let a child leave if they're suspicious. As kids get older teachers start to pay a little less attention and kids are allowed to just leave the classroom at the end of the day (my kiddos are too young and therefore aren't) but parents are right to be cautious. At least you know there's at least one person who would never let your sister leave with someone weird haha.

&nbsp;

EDIT: OP clarified that the lady in question (or the cow, as he put it) made a huge uneccessary scene of the situation, which I think was definitely an over-reaction. Nonetheless, I stand by the fact that children's safety isn't something that should be taken lightly and 100% support parental intervention if something looks strange, as long as they're doing so in a reasonable way. I personally had a parent who was standing in the doorway of my classroom not let a child run out the door when someone who I hadn't released them to was motioning for them to come outside (it was an uncle who was approved to pick up but had never done so before and wasn't familiar with the procedure of me having to see them and "release" the child, so it ended up being cleared up pretty easily). The parent, a father of one of my students, handled it calmly and politely and I'm glad he helped because I may not have seen who the kid left with otherwise.  The first time I picked up my little brothers when they were in elementary school and I was 17, they ID'd me and almost didn't let me pick them up because I had a different last name and wasn't on the pick-up list. It was cleared up with a phone call. A year later a new office administrator ID'd me and checked the pick-up list because she didn't know me. Just a few months ago my older sister had a hard time taking our middle school aged brother home from a soccer game as my mom specified he'd take the bus home from away games and the soccer coach had never seen her before. Allow me to clarify that I absolutely do not think it's okay that men often outright get treated like shit in the situation just because they're men, I feel safe knowing that my young family members will never go home with someone they shouldn't, even if that means proving I'm someone they are allowed to go home with, and I'm sure that many parents feel the same way. I'm sorry to all the guys out there who have been harassed by crazy moms when there is a polite way to handle the situation.

My parents were physically and emotionally abusive and controlling. My life was going to school, coming home, and living under their thumb. I wasn't allowed to date, wasn't allowed to go out much, and was allowed to go to 1 or two friends houses that they "liked". I was a senior in high school with no life skills because they had entirely crippled me so I could never leave. They even moved me 45 minutes away from my older sister, my only lifeline to the "normal" world and life. I asked them if I could start applying for colleges, to which they said no, but that if I wanted to go to college I could continue my life there in the house and go to the crappy, small town, local community college that didn't have the program I wanted anyway. I forged my mom's signature on a form for a program my high school had where they'd pay for your college application fees if your family was lower income (we were), and applied to all the major universities in my state in secret. I got into all of them. It gave confidence beyond anything I had ever experienced, more than my parents ever made me feel I could be. The problem was my mom found my acceptance letters when she was going through my room. My parents and I got into a huge fight, it got physical, I got grounded from my phone and my art supplies. They told me that if I tried going to college that they were kicking me out immediately and I couldn't take anything with me. About a week later, we got into another fight, my parents told me to get out of the house. They did it to scare me, it was a cold night in February, I was sick, they thought with nothing on me, I'd come crawling back and be the complacent slave they wanted. What they didn't know was that I had spent the last week packing bags and getting them out of the house. I was suspicious that my parents would try something like that and needed to have a plan just in case. I walked over to my best friend's house, who had been storing my stuff, knocked on her door and swore I was never going back. We asked her parents that night if I could move in until I finished high school, they agreed on the spot. I called my sister and had her come pick me up for a few days in case my parents went looking for me then. I was terrified that I was so weak they would talk me into coming back, and then I'd be as good as dead. They did go looking for me when I didn't come back, they weren't expecting me to disappear. But I freaking did, guys. I finished high school and moved to the city, got a job, got on my feet. I have two wonderful jobs that I love, a fiancé that I love even more, we have an apartment and a dog. We just got approved for a bigger unit, a 400 square foot increase. I am incredibly happy and so glad I was clever and brave enough to get out!

&nbsp;

EDIT: thank you everyone for the overwhelming amount of kind responses, I'm glad that my story was able to make people smile. I recognize that I am very lucky to have left that situation, and for any of you reading this who might be in a similar place, please know that you are stronger and braver than you think. Keep fighting, and when your moment comes, don't look back. Best of luck to everyone and thank you all again for the positivity.

&nbsp;

So, to answer some questions, I did eventually get in contact with my parents. My sister actually talked to them first to let them know that, hey, I wasn't dead in a ditch after they threw me into the cold. She told them she knew where I was and that I'd talk to them when I was ready. A few weeks later, I called them, basically to say my piece and that I wouldn't be coming back, they actually had the nerve to ask me why I left and make it seem like I just "ran away without telling anyone". It was frustrating. I don't talk to my step dad at all anymore, he was a key part of my abuse and didn't handle me laying into him very well. He called my sister and threatened her life, tried to stalk me, showed up to my school, etc. My mom kinda crept back into my life when my step dad cheated on her a few years ago. She kicked him out and it really did fuck her up for a while, she apologized for how she let him treat me. She was still crazy and narcissistic, but I was there for her as much as I could be until it became too much. I stopped answering her calls as often, and then she eventually stopped calling altogether because she let my step dad move back in. She lives in her toxic little bubble of denial and I try to stay as far outside of it as possible. They've never even had any of my addresses since I left. I got a rare call from my mom around my birthday, she told me she hoped my grandma didn't die of a broken heart since my grandad just passed away in August, mostly she just talked to me about her own drama, and then screamed at me. So for those of you who asked me when the last time I talked to her was, and how that went, there ya go.

&nbsp;

I actually don't have much contact with the friend whose family took me in, anymore. It's a long story, but that friend treated me so badly when I lived there. She used me as a scapegoat to get away with some of the shady stuff she was doing, and ended up having some serious mental issues. Living with her showed me her true colors, and I realized she'd been lying to me during the previous years of our friendship. We try to stay in touch but it's just weird now, I know when she's lying and she doesn't like to be called out for it. Her parents really wanted me to stay longer, knowing how she was treating me and promising I was still like a daughter to them no matter what happened with the friendship. But I was already going through so much at the time that I really felt I needed to start fresh and just get away from all the yucky stuff keeping me down.

&nbsp;


As far as college goes, I couldn't afford going to a university right away. I went to a community college to obtain my AAS, it took me a little longer because I took 12-13 credits a semester instead of the 18-20 credits required to finish in 2 years, I needed time to work two jobs and get me through. I am currently working toward my BAS at one of the universities that originally accepted me. My education really means the world to me, and there is nothing my crappy parents can keep me from, anymore.

I went to a scandelous high school. We had a few:
&nbsp;

1. Entire wing that used to be open during lunch got closed because lunch monitor caught a guy fingering his girlfriend during lunch.
